// The framework's own connect point for who the visitor is. The edge holds the whole
// truth about a session, the browser holds only an opaque cookie, and this is how the
// first reaches the second. An ordinary contract, so session state rides the same
// authenticated wss link as everything else and inherits the upgrade verifier rather
// than opening a second path that would have to re-implement it.
//
// It is hosted on every accepted connection, not only where a project configures a
// sign-in. `Session.scope` and `Session.identity` are not a feature an app turns on. They
// are what the runtime API says a client can always ask, and a channel that exists only
// sometimes is a channel an app finds out about in production.

contract SessionState {
    // Scope and identity together, as JSON:
    //
    //     {"scope": "player", "identity": {"sub": "...", "login": "...", ...}}
    //
    // with `identity` null while anonymous. One property rather than two because QML
    // reads them together (an app gates on `hasScope` and then names the visitor), and
    // two properties would push in two steps with a frame in between where the client is
    // elevated and nameless. The same reason `Pages.routeTable` carries JSON.
    prop string session
}
